<DocsLayout
  title="Storage"
  subtitle="Persistent data layout, migrations, locks, caches, and SQLite stores used by nikcli."
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="overview" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Overview</h2>
    <p>
      nikcli storage is split across a JSON object store and several SQLite databases under the
      global data root. The JSON store is the hot path for project, session, message, part, diff,
      goal, routine, and similar structured records. SQLite is used where indexes, uniqueness,
      credentials, or high-frequency authentication lookups are a better fit.
    </p>
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 my-8">
      <DocCard title="Data root" eyebrow="Global.Path.data">
        <p>Primary persistent root. It can be overridden with <code>NIKCLI_DATA_DIR</code> or isolated in tests with <code>NIKCLI_TEST_HOME</code>.</p>
      </DocCard>
      <DocCard title="JSON store" eyebrow="storage">
        <p>Effect-backed read, write, update, remove, list, and transaction operations over JSON files.</p>
      </DocCard>
      <DocCard title="SQLite DBs" eyebrow="Drizzle and bun:sqlite">
        <p>User accounts, mobile tokens, account credentials, and workspaces use SQLite with WAL and foreign keys.</p>
      </DocCard>
      <DocCard title="Locks" eyebrow="Filesystem">
        <p>Reads and writes use file locks to protect concurrent CLI, server, mobile, and TUI activity.</p>
      </DocCard>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="path-roots" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Path roots</h2>
    <p>
      <code>packages/nikcli/src/global/index.ts</code> owns the global directory model. On startup,
      <code>initialize</code> creates data, cache, config, state, log, bin, and repos directories.
      POSIX systems follow XDG-style roots; Windows uses AppData roots instead of POSIX dotfiles.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Path</th><th>Default role</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>Global.Path.data</code></td><td>Long-lived user data</td><td><code>storage</code>, <code>users.db</code>, <code>accounts.db</code>, <code>mobile_auth.db</code>, <code>workspaces.db</code>, <code>auth.json</code>, <code>snapshot</code>, <code>plans</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Global.Path.bin</code></td><td>nikcli-managed binaries</td><td>LSP downloads, server support binaries, language-server packages.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Global.Path.cache</code></td><td>Disposable cache</td><td>Model lists, package cache, file search indexes. Cache version changes clear this directory.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Global.Path.config</code></td><td>User config</td><td><code>nikcli.json</code>, managed config, global themes, global skills.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Global.Path.state</code></td><td>Ephemeral state</td><td>Locks, prompt stash, plugin metadata, brain lock files.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Global.Path.repos</code></td><td>Hosted repositories</td><td>Imported or managed repositories under the data root.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <DocCode language="bash">
NIKCLI_DATA_DIR=/srv/nikcli-data nikcli serve --hostname 0.0.0.0 --port 4096
NIKCLI_TEST_HOME=/tmp/nikcli-test bun test test/path/file.test.ts
    </DocCode>
  </section>

  <section id="json-store" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>JSON object store</h2>
    <p>
      <code>Storage.Service</code> maps a string-key array to a JSON file below
      <code>Global.Path.data/storage</code>. A key such as
      <code>session / PROJECT_ID / SESSION_ID</code> becomes a file named
      <code>storage/session/PROJECT_ID/SESSION_ID.json</code>. The service is exposed as an Effect
      layer and used through small wrappers in session, mobile, workspace, stats, goals, and summary
      modules.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Implementation behavior</th><th>Typical callers</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>read</code></td><td>Checks the in-memory cache, then takes a read lock and parses JSON.</td><td>Session lookup, message reads, mobile lists, stats aggregation.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>write</code></td><td>Takes a write lock, writes pretty JSON, and updates the cache.</td><td>Session creation, message parts, todos, goals, routine records.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>update</code></td><td>Takes a write lock, clones existing JSON, mutates the draft, writes it back, and caches it.</td><td>Session title, share state, goal state, routine changes.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>remove</code></td><td>Invalidates cache and deletes the JSON file, ignoring only missing-file deletes.</td><td>Session deletion, message deletion, goal cleanup, routine deletion.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>list</code></td><td>Scans all JSON files below a prefix and returns sorted storage keys.</td><td>Session lists, stats, workspace fallback scans, mobile session lists.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>transaction</code></td><td>Invalidates every affected cache key, orders targets, and executes writes or removals under write-lock control.</td><td>Batch storage writes where all operations need to move together.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="session-layout" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Session layout</h2>
    <p>
      Session storage is intentionally normalized. Session metadata lives by project id, messages
      live by session id, and parts live by message id. This keeps transcript reads and part updates
      targeted while allowing all sessions for a project to be listed quickly.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Storage key</th><th>File pattern</th><th>Written by</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>session / PROJECT_ID / SESSION_ID</code></td><td><code>storage/session/PROJECT_ID/SESSION_ID.json</code></td><td><code>Session.create</code>, <code>Session.update</code>, mobile session create, workspace restore.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>message / SESSION_ID / MESSAGE_ID</code></td><td><code>storage/message/SESSION_ID/MESSAGE_ID.json</code></td><td><code>Session.message</code> and <code>MessageV2</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>part / MESSAGE_ID / PART_ID</code></td><td><code>storage/part/MESSAGE_ID/PART_ID.json</code></td><td><code>Session.processor</code>, stream deltas, tool output persistence.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>session_diff / SESSION_ID</code></td><td><code>storage/session_diff/SESSION_ID.json</code></td><td><code>SessionSummary</code> and revert flows.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>goal / SESSION_ID</code></td><td><code>storage/goal/SESSION_ID.json</code></td><td><code>SessionGoal</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>todo / SESSION_ID</code></td><td><code>storage/todo/SESSION_ID.json</code></td><td><code>Todo</code> service and todo tool output.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>routine / PROJECT_ID / ROUTINE_ID</code></td><td><code>storage/routine/PROJECT_ID/ROUTINE_ID.json</code></td><td><code>packages/nikcli/src/mobile/routine.ts</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>session_share / SESSION_ID</code></td><td><code>storage/session_share/SESSION_ID.json</code></td><td>Share lookup path for the session layer.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <DocCallout type="info" title="Plans are separate">
      <p>
        Plan files are markdown, not JSON storage records. Git-backed projects use
        <code>.nikcli/plans</code> in the worktree; non-VCS sessions use the global data
        <code>plans</code> directory.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="sqlite" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>SQLite stores</h2>
    <p>
      The SQLite layer uses <code>bun:sqlite</code>, Drizzle schemas, and startup migrations. Generic
      database opening in <code>packages/nikcli/src/storage/db.ts</code> enables WAL mode,
      <code>synchronous = NORMAL</code>, a busy timeout, cache sizing, and foreign keys before
      applying migration journals when present. Several modules also run direct
      <code>CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS</code> migrations for backward compatibility.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Database</th><th>Owner</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>users.db</code></td><td><code>packages/nikcli/src/db/users.ts</code></td><td>Local users, user sessions, chat contacts, and chat messages.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>accounts.db</code></td><td><code>packages/nikcli/src/account/db.ts</code></td><td>Nikcli account records, access tokens, refresh tokens, and active account config.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>mobile_auth.db</code></td><td><code>packages/nikcli/src/mobile/auth.ts</code></td><td>Hashed mobile bearer tokens with creation, last-used, and expiry timestamps.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>workspaces.db</code></td><td><code>packages/nikcli/src/workspace/db.ts</code></td><td>Workspace records, target config, status, events, and event limits.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The schema re-export in <code>packages/nikcli/src/storage/schema.ts</code> collects table
      definitions from user, account, workspace, and mobile-auth modules so shared tooling can refer
      to the current database surface.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="flow" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Read and write flow</h2>
    <p>
      Most runtime modules do not import filesystem helpers directly. They build small wrappers
      around <code>Storage.defaultLayer</code>, yield <code>Storage.Service</code>, and call the needed
      operation. That keeps storage behavior consistent across CLI, TUI, server routes, mobile
      endpoints, and background services.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Module builds a key</strong> such as <code>message / SESSION_ID / MESSAGE_ID</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>Effect layer loads state</strong> and runs pending JSON migrations once for the store directory.</li>
      <li><strong>Operation acquires a lock</strong> for reads, writes, updates, removes, or batch operations.</li>
      <li><strong>Cache is checked or invalidated</strong> by exact key or prefix depending on the operation.</li>
      <li><strong>JSON is read, written, or listed</strong> beneath <code>Global.Path.data/storage</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>Caller publishes events</strong> when needed, such as <code>session.updated</code> after session updates.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section id="migrations" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Migrations</h2>
    <p>
      JSON migrations live in the <code>MIGRATIONS</code> array inside
      <code>packages/nikcli/src/storage/storage.ts</code>. The store reads a numeric
      <code>storage/migration</code> file, runs each pending migration in order, and writes the next
      index only after the migration succeeds. Failed migrations are logged and do not advance the
      marker.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Migration</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Data touched</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Legacy project import</td><td>Moves old per-project storage into global project, session, message, and part records.</td><td>Old project folders, git root commits, session info, messages, parts.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Session diff extraction</td><td>Moves detailed diffs out of session summaries into <code>session_diff</code> and replaces summary with counts.</td><td><code>session</code> and <code>session_diff</code>.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <DocCallout type="warn" title="Back up before manual edits">
      <p>
        JSON migrations can copy, rewrite, and move session data. Back up the data root before
        manually editing stored files or replaying migration state.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="locks-cache" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Locks and cache</h2>
    <p>
      The JSON store uses an in-memory read-through cache with a default five-second TTL. Writes and
      updates refresh the exact cache key. Removes and transactions invalidate affected keys before
      touching files. The lock layer protects readers and writers that target the same JSON file.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Read lock</td><td>Prevents parsing partially written files during concurrent sessions or server requests.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Write lock</td><td>Serializes updates to the same JSON record.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Cache TTL</td><td>Reduces repeated JSON parsing during list/detail refreshes while keeping recent writes visible.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sorted transaction targets</td><td>Uses stable ordering to reduce deadlock risk when multiple files are touched together.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>NotFoundError</code></td><td>Converts missing-file reads into a typed storage error that server routes can return as HTTP 404.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="operational-checks" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Operational checks</h2>
    <p>Use these checks before deleting data, moving a data root, or debugging missing sessions.</p>
    <DocCode language="bash">
nikcli debug paths
find "$HOME/.local/share/nikcli/storage" -maxdepth 3 -type f | sort | head
sqlite3 "$HOME/.local/share/nikcli/users.db" ".tables"
sqlite3 "$HOME/.local/share/nikcli/mobile_auth.db" "select id, name, expires_at from mobile_tokens;"
    </DocCode>
    <p>
      On macOS or Linux, the default data root usually follows the XDG data location. If
      <code>NIKCLI_DATA_DIR</code> is set, inspect that path instead. On Windows, inspect the nikcli
      directory under AppData Local.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="failure-modes" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Failure modes</h2>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Likely cause</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Session list is empty</td><td>Wrong data root, wrong project id, or sessions stored under another worktree project.</td><td>Check <code>nikcli debug paths</code>, inspect <code>storage/session</code>, and verify the active directory.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>HTTP 404 for a stored object</td><td>Storage read raised <code>NotFoundError</code> for a missing JSON file.</td><td>Confirm the key path exists and that no concurrent cleanup deleted it.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Repeated migration logs</td><td>The migration marker did not advance because a migration failed.</td><td>Read the storage logs, fix the failing source file or permissions, then restart.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>SQLite write waits or fails</td><td>A process holds the database busy longer than the configured timeout, or the filesystem does not support WAL cleanly.</td><td>Close competing processes, check permissions, and verify the database is on a normal local filesystem.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mobile token works once then disappears</td><td>Token expired, was revoked, or the old JSON token file migrated into SQLite and was removed.</td><td>List tokens with <code>nikcli mobile token list</code> and issue a new token when needed.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Manual edits do not appear immediately</td><td>The in-memory cache can serve a value for a short TTL.</td><td>Restart the process or wait for cache expiry after manual file changes.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

